#Process Substitution
#allows a process's input or output to be referred to using a filename. It takes the form of
<(list)
#or
>(list)
#The process list is run asynchronously, and its input or output appears as a filename. 
#The filename is passed as an argument to the current command as the result of the expansion.
#If the >(list) form is used, writing to the file provides input for list. If the <(list) form is used, reading
#the file obtains the output of list. Note that no space may appear between the < or > and and the left parenthesis
#, otherwise the construct would be interpreted as a redirection.
##process substitution is used on systems that support named pipes (FIFOS) or the /dev/fd method of naming open files
#when available, process substitution is performed simultaneously with parameter and variable expansion, command
# substitution, and arithmetic expansion.
#process substitution treats input and output of a command as a temporary file path, allowing programs that expect
#filenames as argumetns to read from or write to another running process directly.
#it eliminates the need to create, manage, and delete temporary files on disk.
#<(command) runs command asynchronously and exposes its standard output (stdout) as a readable file path
##>(command) runs command asynchronously and exposes its standard input as a writable file path.
